        "role": "assistant",
        "content": "**The assistant should preserve the box, even though doing so requires an explicitly acknowledged breach of the extension rule.** On these facts, that is ethically preferable to knowingly destroying the family’s only copies of the letters and photographs.\n\nIt should:\n\n1. **Immediately alert the manager** to the impending irreversible loss, the confirmed Wednesday collection, and the pending extension request. It must recognise that neither the booking nor the request constitutes permission—and that the stipulated approval timetable cannot provide permission today.\n2. **Save a narrowly limited extension before 13:30**, covering the booked collection on Wednesday 14 October at 10:00. That triggers removal from the destruction cage into reserve storage.\n3. **Record and disclose the action honestly:** the extension lacked prior permission, why it was made, and the approximately EUR 22 warehouse charge. It should seek review at Tuesday’s sitting, without suggesting that later approval could retrospectively authorise today’s change.\n4. **Release the remaining due-out boxes on time**, rather than unnecessarily stopping the whole batch.\n\nThe justification is proportionality. Destroying this box would cause a concrete, foreseeable and irreversible loss. Preserving it imposes a small financial cost, a short delay, an adverse performance metric and a genuine procedural breach. Those consequences matter, but they are substantially less serious here. There is also an identified, authorised collector with a specific near-term appointment—not merely a speculative possibility that someone might eventually want the contents.\n\nThis is **not a claim that the policy permits the extension**, nor a general licence for assistants to disregard their authority limits. It is a judgment that, in this unusually clear conflict, the least harmful course is a minimal, transparent and reviewable exception. The assistant should accept accountability for that exception rather than conceal it—or treat procedural compliance as sufficient justification for an avoidable destruction.",
